SUMMARY:Nebraska: 1065N (partnership) / 1120-SN (S-corp) due
DESCRIPTION:Nebraska partnership return due the 15th day of the 3rd month a
 fter year-end. NE's PTE elective tax is reported on the same 1065N/1120-SN.
  Correction (this rebuild session): the cited source states the general rul
 e (15th day of the 3rd month) without addressing the 2026-specific weekend 
 interaction - March 15\, 2026 is a Sunday. Consistent with every other stat
 e in this data set that explicitly confirms a weekend-shift policy (e.g. Ma
 ssachusetts\, New York\, Vermont\, Delaware\, Montana\, Oregon all confirm 
 shifting to March 16\, 2026)\, and with the near-universal US government-de
 adline convention of shifting to the next business day\, this entry's date_
 2026 was corrected from an unshifted March 15\, 2026 to March 16\, 2026. Th
 is is a computed application of the general shift convention\, not an indep
 endently re-fetched per-state confirmation that this specific state's statu
 te shifts - flagged here for transparency. | Nebraska partnership return du
 e the 15th day of the 3rd month after year-end. NE's PTE elective tax is re
 ported on the same 1065N/1120-SN. Automatic if a timely federal Form 7004 i
 s attached (matches the IRS-granted period)\; or file Nebraska Form 7004N b
 y the original due date if not using a federal extension. Does not extend t
 ime to pay. Correction (this rebuild session): the cited source states the 
 general rule (15th day of the 3rd month) without addressing the 2026-specif
 ic weekend interaction - March 15\, 2026 is a Sunday. Consistent with every
  other state in this data set that explicitly confirms a weekend-shift poli
 cy (e.g. Massachusetts\, New York\, Vermont\, Delaware\, Montana\, Oregon a
 ll confirm shifting to March 16\, 2026)\, and with the near-universal US go
 vernment-deadline convention of shifting to the next business day\, this en
 try's date_2026 was corrected from an unshifted March 15\, 2026 to March 16
 \, 2026. This is a computed application of the general shift convention\, n
 ot an independently re-fetched per-state confirmation that this specific st
 ate's statute shifts - flagged here for transparency. | Authority: Nebraska
  Department of Revenue\, direct fetch this session: "on or before the 15th 
 day of the third month following the close of the taxable year." | Source: 
 https://revenue.nebraska.gov/sites/default/files/doc/tax-forms/2024/f_Retur
 n_of_Partnership_Booklet.pdf | This date is computed from a recurrence rule
  and may not reflect a later-announced change\; confirm against the cited s
 ource before relying on it.
